{"agent_id":"pp3g-eCjs-adversarial-review-of-the-135-unified-agent-lifecycle","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T04:41:07.573989516Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"45211db7-6d41-4630-a96a-c9ca201123ae","comment_uuid":"e936a8cf-2304-47a9-9791-1efd3e1065a6","author":"pp3g-eCjs-adversarial-review-of-the-135-unified-agent-lifecycle","content":"[PROGRESS] state=working completed=read #135 including TWO-TIER RECOVERY DESIGN plus specified #133/#134 findings blocker=none next=write one verdict covering timeout ownership, nudge/relaunch safety, stall detection, and missing controls","created_at":"2026-08-03T04:41:07.566993897Z","kind":"observation","signed_by":"SHA256:5ZCoOw8KltFBSSMJh1Kt79ZZ11Hbm+WJ0lDYQUn74HA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgWyypBxlLiXC3R/2hgrR+fWiAWAjGVhtUlNCdj/NLdEIAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAXyHZonAEyaPYfkPsgE12OzPOLH0sExPuxHVh7mohpqzeaTStLwA+qq9XpzKLBnA+oSnMpGvKuCNhpPA2ipZLAQ=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:5ZCoOw8KltFBSSMJh1Kt79ZZ11Hbm+WJ0lDYQUn74HA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgWyypBxlLiXC3R/2hgrR+fWiAWAjGVhtUlNCdj/NLdEIAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQJKc5M2hKpoNMKltsxyNXB/XNsIGa8knMOxBdcePuPAjB76XqpGG2i90OywyZoY6DOZXlT+DaMfqu4GV2Q6HJAk="}
{"agent_id":"pp3g-eCjs-adversarial-review-of-the-135-unified-agent-lifecycle","agent_seq":6,"timestamp":"2026-08-03T04:41:31.991492080Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"45211db7-6d41-4630-a96a-c9ca201123ae","comment_uuid":"980dadaf-45ef-4253-899d-1992efc16571","author":"pp3g-eCjs-adversarial-review-of-the-135-unified-agent-lifecycle","content":"VERDICT — CONDITIONAL: adopt the watcher for observation/notification, but do not enable autonomous recovery until the controls below are specified and fault-tested. (1) Nudge-first is directionally safer than immediate kill+relaunch because it preserves context and avoids needless token burn, but tmux send-keys is not a reliable recovery primitive: it can target the wrong pane, arrive while input is not accepted, duplicate a still-running request, or corrupt a prompt. Require a unique run/session identity, cooldown, an auditable nudge, and a fresh independent activity signal before escalation. Preserve+kill+relaunch is a reasonable last resort only after quiescing/locking the worktree and proving the prior run is dead; automated commit of uncommitted changes is itself race-prone and must not run concurrently with agent writes. (2) Request-level timeout is the correct root-cause tier if the hang is an unbounded provider stream. It is feasible only if implemented at the actual provider/stream boundary with cancellation propagation, bounded connect/idle/total deadlines, and explicit retry classification. Verify the OpenCode/provider client path rather than assuming AI_RetryError covers this case. Test partial streams, tool calls, non-idempotent operations, cancellation, and duplicate retry behavior; otherwise Tier 1 can create duplicate work or merely move the hang. (3) The >2x expected-interval rule is a useful alert heuristic, not a reliable state transition by itself. ‘Expected interval’ is underspecified and fixed thresholds will false-positive during long compiles/tools, approvals, provider slowness, timer jitter, and sync delays. Use monotonic timestamps, an independent heartbeat/activity contract, phase-specific/adaptive deadlines, and grace periods; require status nonterminal plus stale heartbeat/activity, and record why a run is exempt. File mtimes/commits/comments alone are weak signals because a hung request may have no writes and a slow valid operation may also have none. (4) Main risks are real: relaunch can mask provider/systemic incidents and burn tokens; duplicate agents can race for the same issue/worktree; preserve/commit can capture partial or conflicting state; tmux/process identity can be stale; hydration/sync can observe stale status or race with launch/cleanup; and notifications can be lost. Max-two relaunches limits loops but does not prevent two watchers/runs from acting on the same agent, nor does it define whether the counter is per issue, run, or incident. (5) Missing gates: atomic status schema with run ID and timestamps; single-writer/lease ownership and idempotency keys; recovery ledger recording detection, nudge, kill, relaunch, and operator notification outcomes; launch/kill confirmation and duplicate-run prevention; backoff/jitter and a global concurrency/token budget; explicit handling for human intervention, CI_FAILED, removed worktrees, sync outages, and watcher restart; secure/validated tmux target and command quoting; notification failure escalation; dry-run/manual-approval mode; fault-injection tests for hung streams and slow-but-working agents; and a documented, explicitly approved exception to HALT-on-failure. Recommendation: ship monitor + notify + request-timeout instrumentation first; make nudge/relaunch opt-in or manual until these invariants are demonstrated.","created_at":"2026-08-03T04:41:31.974791872Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:5ZCoOw8KltFBSSMJh1Kt79ZZ11Hbm+WJ0lDYQUn74HA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgWyypBxlLiXC3R/2hgrR+fWiAWAjGVhtUlNCdj/NLdEIAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABA013MNQWTRtlhYUx+iCrHl8Ql9mi6tOR/0VC2QtyeukHujkB65lEkRxwQszKcyUU7GdmTXyvDBRatWcSx63xOAg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:5ZCoOw8KltFBSSMJh1Kt79ZZ11Hbm+WJ0lDYQUn74HA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgWyypBxlLiXC3R/2hgrR+fWiAWAjGVhtUlNCdj/NLdEIAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQAdPdMBKWus4PIjUHpgwq1hPtEDRqRPVhVDYgsKTSho+f3OJUHf7v61hV/5WoxKpRA/NsnXCa/ez7Qww+bWn3Ak="}
